WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.130
Two plans for exam week land on my desk.

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.860
One finishes in four days, but nine

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.960
pupils sit two exams on the same day.

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.490
The other takes six days, and

5
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.010
only one pupil does.

6
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.900
Which plan is better?

7
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.200
Today: two goals at once, and how to

8
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.840
choose between them in the open.

9
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.590
When two goals pull apart,

10
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.630
no plan wins on both.

11
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.710
A shorter exam week means more

12
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.830
pupils with two exams in a day.

13
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.320
A gentler week takes longer.

14
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.260
That’s a trade-off: to gain on one

15
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.200
goal, you give up some of the other.

16
00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.950
Ms Salma lists six plans and drops any

17
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.050
plan that another plan beats: no worse on

18
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.970
either goal, and better on one.

19
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.260
Plan C takes five days with six

20
00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.120
pupils clashing, but plan B also

21
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.020
takes five days, with only four.

22
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.500
So C goes.

23
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.930
What’s left, plans A, B and

24
00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.990
D, is the Pareto front.

25
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.180
A plan is on the Pareto front when no

26
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.400
other plan is at least as good on

27
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.660
every goal and better on one.

28
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.320
Picking from the front

29
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.740
isn’t maths any more.

30
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.320
It’s a judgement about what matters more.

31
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.590
One way to choose is to give

32
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.930
each goal a weight.

33
00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.310
Count one exam day as much as one

34
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.990
clashing pupil, and plan D wins:

35
00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.190
six plus one is seven.

36
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.680
Count a day as much as four clashing

37
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.320
pupils, and plan B wins instead.

38
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.880
So the weights decide, and

39
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.120
weights are values, not facts.

40
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.480
Agree them before you see the plans,

41
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.560
with the people who carry the cost:

42
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.180
teachers, pupils, parents.

43
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.070
Then say them out loud.

44
00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:57.940
Quick check!

45
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.210
Plan E takes seven days,

46
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.650
with one pupil clashing.

47
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.280
Plan D takes six days, also with one.

48
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.630
Can E ever be the right choice?

49
00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.230
No: D is never worse and it’s shorter, so

50
00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.210
E is beaten, whatever the weights.

51
00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.500
Now a different trade-off.

52
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.780
What is one more unit of a limit worth?

53
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.250
In the school garden, the best plan

54
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:29.750
earns two hundred and ninety.

55
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.270
Give pupils one more hour a week, solve

56
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.750
again, and it earns two hundred and

57
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.510
ninety-five: an hour is worth five.

58
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.650
One more bed of land is worth twenty-five.

59
00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.590
And more water is worth nothing,

60
00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.910
because three cans are already spare.

61
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.130
In the app, open the garden plan.

62
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.480
Raise the tomato price and

63
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.060
watch the best corner jump.

64
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.260
Then give pupils more hours, and

65
00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.040
see what the extra time buys.

66
00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:00.910
Your turn.

67
00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.080
The garden club can buy one more bed of

68
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.280
land for twenty coins, or pay for one

69
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.440
more hour of pupils’ time for ten.

70
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.260
Which is worth buying?

71
00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.610
The bed: it adds twenty-five for twenty

72
00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.070
coins, while an hour adds only five for

73
00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:19.270
ten.

74
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.100
Remember: when goals pull apart,

75
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.300
keep the plans nobody beats.

76
00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.930
Weights pick a plan, so agree them openly.

77
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.180
And solve again to see

78
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.100
what a limit is worth.

79
00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.650
Try it in class tomorrow: give

80
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.450
pupils four plans for sports

81
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.370
day, each with two scores.

82
00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.070
Groups cross out every beaten plan.

83
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.040
Then each group argues for one of the

84
00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.600
rest, and says which goal it weighted

85
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:47.960
more.
